G5.3b.7 slice 5 entry design:

- The topology owner is structured concurrency, not another state authority.
  It owns the runtime clone, one cancellation domain, every task handle and the
  sole publisher claim. It owns no transaction, queue, retry ledger, health
  mirror or repair protocol. A caller cannot close effects or detach a worker
  independently of this owner in production.
- Startup performs every fallible step before the first task is spawned:
  prepare the optional template adapter, construct endpoint workers, claim the
  sole effect consumer and reserve the exact verifier handle population. After
  that point, spawning the cache consumer, publisher, compute drivers and
  independent template lanes is an infallible ownership transfer. Any startup
  error drops the claim and leaves zero partial topology.
- Normal shutdown is one capability proof: close ordinary ingress and ordered
  chain/admin producers outside this isolated slice; cancel compute, Ready,
  maintenance and template producers; join compute producers so all checked
  out leases settle or become typed stale; close effect production only after
  active work reaches zero; drain and join the uncancellable sole publisher;
  close and drain cache updates; then permit one coherent persistence capture.
  No authority/output/cache lock crosses an await.
- A task return is classified by semantic ownership, never by `is_finished`:

  | Boundary result | Topology disposition | Persistence |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | cancellation or closed producer input after shutdown begins | clean join | eligible after effect drain |
  | stale, bounded backpressure or allocation pressure | handled inside the driver by level re-read/backoff | unchanged |
  | template read/build/publication failure | retain the last valid template and wait for a later source cut; report derived degradation | eligible |
  | verification-cache worker/channel failure | discard only best-effort cache publication; validation never trusts an unbound cache result | eligible |
  | callback/network/recent-reject timeout or failure | existing bounded per-endpoint circuit disposal | eligible |
  | relay receiver disconnection | permanently circuit-dispose that external endpoint for this topology and continue draining committed effects | eligible |
  | authority `Fault` proving ownership/charge/index/scheduler/dependency/effect contradiction | invalidate the current generation; do not reinterpret as transaction rejection | forbidden |
  | failed compute/effect settlement still owning the sole capability and not provably stale/retryable | invalidate the current generation because safe continuation cannot discharge authoritative ownership | forbidden |
  | `JoinError` from an authority-owning worker | capability ownership is unknown; invalidate the current generation | forbidden |
  | `JoinError` from template/cache derived work | report degradation only; it cannot decide membership or persistence | eligible |

- Relay disconnection is specifically not a service fault. The sender can
  never reconnect, so retaining the effect head would only deadlock capacity
  and shutdown. Circuit disposal is safe because the absent consumer owns no
  surviving known-filter state; a future relayer instance must establish a new
  endpoint and reconcile from a generation reset at assembly.
- Capability-bearing failure values may not be logged and dropped by a generic
  supervisor. The shutdown report owns them until it either proves staleness,
  completes the settlement, or marks the generation persistence-ineligible.
  This is the sole exceptional generation boundary; no panic catch, state
  repair, worker restart loop or second authority is introduced.
- The isolated topology does not yet switch `TxPoolServiceBuilder`. G5.3c will
  make the service dispatcher select on its event-driven completion boundary,
  close external producers first and consume the typed shutdown report. It may
  not restore the legacy `any_finished() => stop` rule.

